{"abstract":"Purpose: To analyze and interpret why some individuals are resilient to ELS while others are susceptible, resulting in psychiatric outcome later in life, with a focus on the role of DNAm of the NR3C1 gene as a mediating mechanism between ELS and the risk of psychiatric outcomes. We hypothesized that a high level of mental resilience to ELS, expressed as lower incidence of psychiatric outcomes, was associated with attenuated NR3C1 DNAm levels. Materials and methods: The first authors conducted a systematic search on PubMed to identify primary research studies. Abstract were screened and full-text were reviewed to assess the eligibility for inclusion. Consensus on assessment was reached after discussion of eligibility criteria. Studies were sorted based on whether they investigated the association between ELS and NR3C1 DNAm in 1) individuals exposed compared to unexposed to ELS both without a psychiatric outcome or in 2) individuals exposed to ELS with a psychiatric outcome compared to exposed individuals without a psychiatric outcome. Results and conclusion: Seven studies met the eligibility criteria. The results were inconsistent; two studies supported our hypothesis, two studies indicated that increased NR3C1 DNAm mediated resilience to ELS, and three studies found no association.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Forum, Ditte Mathilde Klith","Bjerregaard, Camilla","Thomsen, Per Hove"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-806901"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Bjerregaard, Camilla","Forum, Ditte Mathilde Klith"],"keyword":["DNA methylation","Early life stress","Resilience","psychiatric diagnosis"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the significance of dna methylation of the nr000000000003c000000000001 gene encoding the glucocorticoid receptor for developing resilience in individuals exposed to early life stress","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08039488.2024.2436987","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-806901","identifier":[{"id":"39773140","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85214274550","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-8667debc-de43-47a9-a1cf-28a184ead29a"],"keyword":["DNA methylation","NR3C1 gene","early life stress","psychiatric diagnosis","resilience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E7D77F15C80334CE00E24AA348E2E3BC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08039488.2024.2436987","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Psychiatric Hospital for Children and Adolescents"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ditte Mathilde Klith","key":"forumpau","last":"Forum","name":"Forum, Ditte Mathilde Klith","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Camilla","key":"bjerregaardpau","last":"Bjerregaard","name":"Bjerregaard, Camilla","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Per Hove","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"47bb4a18-534a-4e17-b016-4f42a4e97316","name":"Thomsen, Per Hove","orcid":"0000-0002-4529-4431","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39773140"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/08039488.2024.2436987","issn":["08039488","08039496"],"issn_org":["08039488"],"issue":"1","journal":"Nordic Journal of Psychiatry","pages":"1-14","publisher":"Taylor and Francis Ltd.","status":"p","title":"Nordic Journal of Psychiatry","volume":"79"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85214274550"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-8667debc-de43-47a9-a1cf-28a184ead29a"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"The significance of DNA methylation of the NR3C1 gene encoding the glucocorticoid receptor for developing resilience in individuals exposed to early life stress","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-806901","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background: Vitamin K is a cofactor necessary for the biological activity of proteins like Matrix Gla Protein (MGP), which reduce calcification and help preserve lung function. This study aims to determine, first, whether low vitamin K status is associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), and secondary, whether the level of vitamin K is associated with COPD severity, smoking exposure, or mortality. Methods: The plasma concentration of dephosphorylated uncarboxylated (dp-uc) MGP was used as an inverse biomarker for vitamin K in 98 COPD patients from the CODEX-P COPD study and 986 controls from the DanFunD study. Low vitamin K status was defined as the upper quartile of dp-ucMGP (>589 pmol/L). Using a logistic regression model, we examined whether low vs. high/moderate vitamin K status increased the odds ratio (OR) of having COPD. Secondary analyses, in the COPD cohort only, examined the association between low vitamin K status and COPD severity, smoking exposure in packyears and all-cause mortality, using a Welch’s t-test and log-rank test, respectively. Results: Low vitamin K status was associated with increased odds of having COPD, OR 9.7 (95% CI [5.5 to 17.5], p < 0.001). We found no associations between low vitamin K and COPD severity (est. −0.03, p = 0.7; 95% CI [−0.2 to 0.1]), smoking exposure (p = 0.7), or all-cause mortality (p = 0.5). Conclusions: Low vitamin K status was associated with substantially higher odds of having COPD compared to high/moderate vitamin K status. No association was found between low vitamin K status and COPD severity, smoking exposure, or all-cause mortality. Further studies are needed to determine if vitamin K plays a role in the pathophysiology of COPD and whether supplement therapy is indicated.","author_count":24,"contributors":["Ackermann, Daniel Alexander","Linneberg, Allan","Rastoder, Ema","Vognsen, Anna Kubel","Bjerregaard, Anne Ahrendt","Friis-Hansen, Lennart","Jørgensen, Niklas Rye","Hedsund, Caroline Emma","Johansen, Niklas Dyrby","Modin, Daniel","Dons, Maria","Højbjerg Lassen, Mats C.","Grundtvig Skaarup, Kristoffer","Vesterlev, Ditte","Moberg, Mia","Janner, Julie","Eklöf, Josefin","Pedersen, Lars","Bendstrup, Elisabeth","Laursen, Christian B.","Carlsen, Jørn","Biering-Sørensen, Tor","Jensen, Jens Ulrik Stæhr","Sivapalan, Pradeesh"],"cuid":["S03-099.97-804234"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Ackermann, Daniel Alexander","Sivapalan, Pradeesh"],"keyword":["Biomarker","COPD","Vitamin K","dp ucMGP"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"low vitamin k status and risk of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/biomedicines13040807","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-099.97-804234","identifier":[{"id":"40299372","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105003578012","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC12024738","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-d0c44f4e-0ab0-4361-bea6-c6fb0c287400","ku-383ba3f4-663a-42f9-b1ab-6154f0687555","sdu-c5b18680-f4bd-48f3-9f05-0831bab60efe"],"keyword":["COPD","biomarker","dp-ucMGP","vitamin K"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-A139EC7A3D1AF768D82815FA8DFBBD87"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/biomedicines13040807","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/4489